---
layout: layouts/base.njk
pagination:
  data: activeLangs
  size: 1
  alias: lang
pageSlug: "contact"
pageTitle: contact
---
{% set t = i18n[lang] %}
<header class="page"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="cue rise">{{ t.contact.cue }}</div>
  <h2 class="rise-2">{{ t.contact.h2_thin }} <b>{{ t.contact.h2_bold }}</b></h2>
  <p class="lede rise-3">{{ t.contact.lede }}</p>
</div></header>

<section><div class="wrap">
  {#- POSTS TO THE WORKER, not to Netlify.

     This carried data-netlify="true" and posted back to its own page path,
     which is how Netlify Forms worked on the old host. Netlify intercepted
     that POST; Cloudflare does not, so it reached the static asset handler and
     came back 405. The form looked complete and every submission was lost.

     workers/src/contact-form.js was written to replace it — same field names,
     same bot-field honeypot, and it redirects back with ?sent=true so the
     success message below keeps working unchanged. It was finished and the
     form was never pointed at it. -#}
  <form class="contact" name="contact" method="POST" action="/api/contact">
    <p hidden><label>Do not fill: <input name="bot-field"></label></p>
    <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="{{ t.contact.form_name }}" required>
    <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="{{ t.contact.form_email }}" required>
    {#- THE REASONS COME FROM THE DATABASE, so they cannot be rendered here:
       this page is built by CI, which has no database. The select is filled by
       the script below from /api/contact, and stays hidden if that returns
       nothing — a page with no dropdown still works, a dropdown that leads
       nowhere does not.

       Deliberately the API and not the embed widget: this form already has the
       site's own markup and styling, and the widget would replace something
       that fits with something that only resembles it. The API is the shared
       part; the appearance stays the page's own. -#}
    <select name="topic" id="contact-reason" hidden></select>
    <input type="text" name="subject" maxlength="160"
           placeholder="{{ t.contact.form_subject }}">
    <textarea name="message" placeholder="{{ t.contact.form_message }}" required></textarea>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:10px">
      <button class="btn solid" type="submit">{{ t.contact.form_send }}</button>
    </div>
  </form>
  <p class="legal-note" id="sent-note" style="display:none">{{ t.contact.form_success }}</p>
  {#- A REFUSED SUBMISSION HAS TO SAY SO. The handler redirects back with
     ?error=1 when a field does not pass, and nothing here read it — so a
     visitor who mistyped their address landed on an ordinary-looking contact
     page with their message gone and no idea it had not been sent. The one
     failure mode a contact form must never have. -#}
  <p class="legal-note contact-error" id="error-note" style="display:none">{{ t.contact.form_error }}</p>
  <script>
    var q = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
    if (q.get('sent')) {
      document.getElementById('sent-note').style.display = 'block';
    }
    if (q.get('error')) {
      document.getElementById('error-note').style.display = 'block';
    }

    /* The reasons, fetched rather than built in. A failure here leaves the
       form exactly as it was — the dropdown is an improvement to it, never a
       dependency of it. */
    (function () {
      var sel = document.getElementById('contact-reason');
      if (!sel) return;
      fetch('/api/contact', { headers: { Accept: 'application/json' } })
        .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : null; })
        .then(function (body) {
          var topics = (body && body.topics) || [];
          if (!topics.length) return;
          sel.innerHTML =
            '<option value="">{{ t.contact.form_reason | escape }}</option>' +
            topics.map(function (t) {
              var o = document.createElement('option');
              o.value = t.id;
              o.textContent = t.label;
              return o.outerHTML;
            }).join('');
          sel.hidden = false;
        })
        .catch(function () {});
    })();
  </script>
</div></section>
